    <summary>Self-hosted web interface and REST API for Active Directory and OpenLDAP.</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[Nelsar.Axia is a self-hosted web interface and REST API for managing Active Directory, Samba AD, OpenLDAP and other LDAP directories from the browser: users, groups, organizational units and computer accounts, with role-based access mapped from directory groups, an audit log and bulk provisioning.

This package installs the Windows service `NelsarAxia` from the official MSI. The API and its console are served on one port, 8080 by default. Settings, keys, the database and logs live in `%ProgramData%\Nelsar.Axia` and are kept on upgrade and uninstall.

## Package parameters

Each parameter is passed to the MSI as the property of the same meaning. A parameter that is not given leaves the setting as it is.

* `/Port:` - the port the service listens on (default 8080)
* `/LdapServer:` - the directory server, such as `dc01.corp.example.com`
* `/LdapBaseDn:` - the base DN, such as `DC=corp,DC=example,DC=com`
* `/LdapUsername:` - the bind account, such as `svc-axia@corp.example.com`
* `/AdminGroup:` - the DN of the directory group that gets the Admin role
* `/DatabaseProvider:` - `Sqlite` (default) or `PostgreSql`
* `/StartService` - start the service when the install finishes

Example: `choco install nelsar-axia --params "'/LdapServer:dc01.corp.example.com /LdapBaseDn:DC=corp,DC=example,DC=com /StartService'"`

The bind password is never taken as a parameter, because command lines end up in logs. Add it to `%ProgramData%\Nelsar.Axia\appsettings.json` after installing; see https://nelsar.github.io/Axia/installation-windows/.

64-bit Windows only.]]></description>
    <releaseNotes>https://github.com/Nelsar/Axia/releases/tag/v0.3.0</releaseNotes>
